ablocks - i3bar status command with animated blocks
====================================================
A small i3blocks replacement in the suckless style: blocks are compiled in
from config.h, there is no runtime config file, and the only dependency is
libc. Block scripts written for i3blocks run unchanged.


Requirements
------------
A C99 compiler and make. i3bar to display the output.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your setup (ablocks is installed into the
/usr/local namespace by default), then:

    make clean install

The first build copies config.def.h to config.h; edit config.h and
rebuild to change the blocks.


Running ablocks
---------------
In the bar section of the i3 config:

    status_command ablocks


Blocks
------
Each block runs its command through /bin/sh on its interval (Once = at
startup only), asynchronously, with BLOCK_NAME set. Output is read the
i3blocks way:

    line 1   full_text
    line 2   short_text
    line 3   color
    @...     reserved for animation directives

Exit code 33 marks the block urgent. A block with empty output is hidden.


Animations
----------
An animation in config.h is a list of pango frames wrapped around a
block's output, "%s" standing for the output itself. Use alpha (fades
without touching the script's own colours) and rise (moves the text
without changing its width), so the bar never shifts while one plays.

Each time ablocks starts, i.e. on every i3 restart, bootanim plays across
the bar as a wave, one block after another from left to right. Replay it
with:

    pkill -USR2 -x ablocks

bootdelay gives the blocks time to print before the wave starts and
bootstagger sets the gap between neighbours; keep it a multiple of the
frame time so frames change together. Set bootanim to NULL to disable.
